WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.455
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level C one.

2
00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:08.955
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.255 --> 00:00:15.623
Unit twenty: Advanced Future Forms. Today you will express imminent and scheduled events in formal style.

4
00:00:15.923 --> 00:00:18.552
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.779
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.617 --> 00:00:24.745
Be about to.

7
00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:28.458
Be about to means an action that will happen very soon.

8
00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.620
The film is about to start.

9
00:00:31.217 --> 00:00:33.927
Your turn. Say: Be about to.

10
00:00:36.767 --> 00:00:38.128
Be about to.

11
00:00:38.883 --> 00:00:39.861
Be due to.

12
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.616
Be due to means an event expected at a set time.

13
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:46.344
The train is due to arrive at six.

14
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:49.672
Your turn. Say: Be due to.

15
00:00:52.533 --> 00:00:53.523
Be due to.

16
00:00:54.283 --> 00:00:55.633
Be on the verge of.

17
00:00:56.617 --> 00:00:59.464
Be on the verge of means very close to doing something.

18
00:01:00.050 --> 00:01:01.864
She is on the verge of tears.

19
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.567
Your turn. Say: Be on the verge of.

20
00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:10.085
Be on the verge of.

21
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:11.898
Be set to.

22
00:01:12.883 --> 00:01:15.974
Be set to means expected or scheduled, in news style.

23
00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:18.711
The vote is set to take place Monday.

24
00:01:19.317 --> 00:01:21.876
Your turn. Say: Be set to.

25
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:25.839
Be set to.

26
00:01:26.583 --> 00:01:27.689
Be to.

27
00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:30.950
Be to means an official plan or order.

28
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:33.666
The president is to visit Japan.

29
00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.670
Your turn. Say: Be to.

30
00:01:39.533 --> 00:01:40.558
Be to.

31
00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:42.213
Imminent.

32
00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:45.007
Imminent means about to happen.

33
00:01:45.617 --> 00:01:46.838
An imminent storm.

34
00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:49.533
Your turn. Say: Imminent.

35
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:53.192
Imminent.

36
00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:54.951
Scheduled.

37
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.576
Scheduled means planned for a certain time.

38
00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:00.533
A scheduled meeting.

39
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:03.746
Your turn. Say: Scheduled.

40
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.555
Scheduled.

41
00:02:08.317 --> 00:02:09.062
Formal.

42
00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.933
Formal means serious and official.

43
00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.027
A formal announcement.

44
00:02:14.617 --> 00:02:16.805
Your turn. Say: Formal.

45
00:02:19.667 --> 00:02:20.517
Formal.

46
00:02:21.333 --> 00:02:24.856
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:25.717 --> 00:02:29.539
Visal, how would a newsreader say The president will visit Japan next week?

48
00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:39.978
The president is to visit Japan next week, or is set to visit. Be to and be set to sound official and are common in headlines and announcements.

49
00:02:41.083 --> 00:02:43.141
What about something happening right now?

50
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:54.832
Be about to means very soon: The film is about to start. Be on the verge of means almost at the point of: The company is on the verge of collapse.

51
00:02:55.917 --> 00:02:57.603
What about timetables?

52
00:02:58.717 --> 00:03:12.819
Be due to shows an expected time: The train is due to arrive at six. It is an expectation, not a promise, so delays are possible. Compare be bound to, which is certain.

53
00:03:13.967 --> 00:03:15.432
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:16.283 --> 00:03:21.986
Grammar focus: Advanced future: be about to and due to and set to and to plus infinitive.

55
00:03:22.286 --> 00:03:23.555
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:25.727
The film is about to start.

57
00:03:26.617 --> 00:03:28.837
The train is due to arrive at six.

58
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.481
The president is set to visit Japan next week.

59
00:03:33.433 --> 00:03:41.029
Use be about to for the immediate future, be due to for expected times, be set to / be to for official plans, and be on the verge of for the brink of change.

60
00:03:41.783 --> 00:03:45.411
Be careful! Don't say: The film is going start now.

61
00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:49.552
Say: The film is about to start.

62
00:03:49.852 --> 00:03:51.306
Use be about to.

63
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:54.706
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.791
Breaking News.

65
00:03:57.091 --> 00:03:59.962
The prime minister is set to announce a new policy.

66
00:04:00.967 --> 00:04:03.268
The new bridge is due to open in March.

67
00:04:04.267 --> 00:04:06.951
Talks are about to begin between the two leaders.

68
00:04:07.967 --> 00:04:10.268
The company is on the verge of collapse.

69
00:04:11.267 --> 00:04:14.032
These future forms suit the language of news.

70
00:04:15.133 --> 00:04:18.973
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:19.817 --> 00:04:22.188
The train is blank to arrive at six.

72
00:04:22.488 --> 00:04:26.037
A, due. B, about. C, verge.

73
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:31.321
The answer is A: due.

74
00:04:32.483 --> 00:04:34.390
Be blank to means very soon.

75
00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:37.798
A, bound. B, about. C, due.

76
00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:43.112
The answer is B: about.

77
00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.576
The president is blank visit Japan. (official plan)

78
00:04:47.876 --> 00:04:50.844
A, to. B, for. C, at.

79
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.016
The answer is A: to.

80
00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.556
Which phrase means 'almost at the point of'?

81
00:04:59.856 --> 00:05:04.078
A, set for. B, on the verge of. C, due to.

82
00:05:07.633 --> 00:05:09.517
The answer is B: on the verge of.

83
00:05:10.683 --> 00:05:13.368
According to the reading, when is the bridge due to open?

84
00:05:13.668 --> 00:05:17.484
A, in March. B, in June. C, next year.

85
00:05:21.033 --> 00:05:22.882
The answer is A: in March.

86
00:05:24.067 --> 00:05:27.032
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:27.883 --> 00:05:31.183
Write a news-style sentence using 'be set to' or 'be to'.

88
00:05:39.983 --> 00:05:41.646
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:41.946 --> 00:05:53.262
The new airport is set to open next spring. The mayor is to cut the ribbon at the ceremony. These future forms sound official and suit a news report.

90
00:05:53.562 --> 00:05:55.283
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:05:56.183 --> 00:06:03.306
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and advanced future: be about to and due to and set to and to plus infinitive.

92
00:06:03.606 --> 00:06:07.709
Today's value is foresight. Prepare today for the world of tomorrow.

93
00:06:08.009 --> 00:06:10.685
See you in Unit twenty-one. Goodbye!

94
00:06:10.985 --> 00:06:13.683
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

